proc: interface settings and ping_group_range per network namespace

What:
- /proc/sys/net/ipv4/conf and /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf have, besides all and
  default, a directory for each interface of the network namespace of the
  task that looks it up, as Linux registers one for each device in its
  namespace (devinet_sysctl_register(), addrconf_sysctl_register()) and
  resolves /proc/sys/net in current's namespace (net_ctl_header_lookup()). An
  open file keeps the namespace it was opened in, and so does the directory
  of an interface: reached through a file descriptor of it kept across
  setns(2), its settings are of the namespace it was looked up in, as Linux's
  proc_sys_lookup() finds a directory's entries in its own sysctl header,
  while a path looked up again from another namespace is that namespace's
  (proc_sys_compare(), net_ctl_is_seen()). The directory of an
  interface holds the settings that container engines and network plugins
  write: for IPv4 accept_local, accept_redirects, arp_announce, arp_ignore,
  arp_notify, forwarding, proxy_arp, route_localnet, rp_filter and
  send_redirects, for IPv6 accept_dad, accept_ra, accept_redirects, autoconf,
  disable_ipv6 and forwarding, with Linux's defaults. forwarding is the NIC's
  own forwarding in netstack; the others keep the value written, per
  interface, and change nothing, as netstack implements none of them. They
  are dropped when the interface is removed or leaves the namespace, as Linux
  drops a device's configuration then.
- /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ping_group_range is the opener's namespace's, written as
  Linux's ipv4_ping_group_range() takes it: two group IDs from 0 to GID_T_MAX
  in the writer's user namespace, a value not given kept, an empty range
  stored as "1 0", read in the reader's user namespace. gVisor lets every
  group create ping sockets, so every namespace starts at Linux's largest
  range, "0 4294967294", and the range is not checked when a ping socket is
  created.
- A writer with CAP_NET_ADMIN in the user namespace that owns the network
  namespace of the setting gets the owner's access, as in net/sysctl_net.c:
  net_ctl_permissions(), and access is checked again at every read and write,
  as fs/proc/proc_sysctl.c:proc_sys_call_handler() checks sysctl_perm()
  (EPERM): a file that a privileged process opened is not written through by
  one that dropped its privileges.

Why: Podman's containers.conf sets net.ipv4.ping_group_range to "0 0" in
every container's OCI spec, and crun fails a container whose sysctl it cannot
write: "crun: open `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/ping_group_range`: No such file or
directory". netavark writes rp_filter and route_localnet of its bridge and
arp_notify and rp_filter of the container's interface, from inside the
container's namespace, and fails if one is missing: "netavark: Sysctl error:
no such sysctl: /proc/sys/net/ipv4/conf/podman0/rp_filter" (1.14), "set
sysctl net/ipv4/conf/eth0/arp_notify: ... No such file or directory" (1.17);
1.4 writes route_localnet of the bridge when a port is published. Docker skips
a setting that is missing; its nested checks pass with them as without.

Not done: the other files of /proc/sys/net, all and default among them, are
still those of the root network namespace (gvisor.dev/issue/1833), and an
interface's directory holds only the settings above. Only the directory of an
interface keeps its namespace: one of /proc/sys/net/ipv4/conf, or of
/proc/sys/net/ipv4 for ping_group_range, finds its entries in the namespace
of the task that walks it. gVisor's other sysctl files, as upstream, check
access when they are opened only. Checkpoint and restore of the written
values were not tested.

Upstream: upstream PR 16279 and PR 17148, stacked on it (open on 2026-11-17),
add ip_unprivileged_port_start and ping_group_range per network namespace and
enforce them; this patch takes the same per-namespace file without the
enforcement, which would change what every sandbox may do. None adds the
interface directories. Drop the ping_group_range part when upstream has it.

Verified:
- The new syscall tests ProcSysNet.InterfaceConf (the directories of a new
  namespace, values written and read back, forwarding, another namespace's
  defaults), ProcSysNet.PingGroupRange, .InterfaceConfThroughDirectoryFD
  (reads and writes through a directory kept across namespaces) and
  .WriteChecksPermissionsAgain (a process that dropped to uid 65534 writes
  through a descriptor opened as root: EPERM) pass on Linux 7.0 under runc
  and on runsc with this patch, under the runtime arguments of runsc and of
  runsc-nested; the first two fail on ALE's 0.1.2, the last two on ALE's
  0.1.3 before they were fixed. The whole proc_net_test passes (29
  tests), as does //pkg/sentry/fsimpl/proc:proc_test.
- crun started Podman's containers, and netavark 1.4.0, 1.14.0 and 1.17.2
  set their sysctls, in sandboxes under runsc-nested.

